  "image": "none",
  "questionText": "Find the area bounded by the curve $y = x^2 - 4x + 3$ and the $x$-axis between $x = 0$ and $x = 3$. Show all working.",
  "solution": "Roots: $x^2 - 4x + 3 = (x-1)(x-3) = 0$, so $x = 1$ and $x = 3$. On $[0,1]$ the curve is above the axis; on $[1,3]$ it is below. Area $= \\int_0^1 (x^2 - 4x + 3)\\,dx + \\left|\\int_1^3 (x^2 - 4x + 3)\\,dx\\right|$. Antiderivative: $\\frac{x^3}{3} - 2x^2 + 3x$. First part: $\\frac{1}{3} - 2 + 3 = \\frac{4}{3}$. Second: $\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3} - 2x^2 + 3x\\right]_1^3 = (9 - 18 + 9) - \\frac{4}{3} = -\\frac{4}{3}$, absolute value $\\frac{4}{3}$. Total area $= \\frac{8}{3}$.",
  "tags": [
   "calculus",
   "integration",
   "area",
   "long answer"
  ]
 },
